From f0ebaf67b806eac5c0274d56611dc74d9f517365 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 21:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] PS4: recompress ASTC in slabs, not in one block of the whole
 level

0.9.8. With astc_recompress = bc1 or bc3, ConvertImage decoded a whole level to RGBA8 (every layer of an array, every slice of a 3D image) in
one heap block, then compressed that block. For a 2048 x 2048 image of 6 layers that block is 96 MiB; for 121 layers of the same size it is
more than 800 MiB. The log of Super Mario Maker 2 of 19/11 (astc_recompress = bc1) has "largest single request 100663296 bytes" = 2048 * 2048 * 4 * 6,
a freeze of 5.7 s and the heap growing by 650 MiB in 0.4 s just before std::bad_alloc; the C heap on this port does not give carved-out memory back,
so a peak stays. (That the 96 MiB request is this block is an inference from its size: that log has no symbols for the caller.)

ASTC::Decompress and BCN::CompressBC1/3 both loop over slices that do not depend on each other, so the level is now decoded and compressed a slab at
a time: a few whole slices (up to 8 MiB of RGBA8), or, when one slice alone is bigger, bands of rows that start on a multiple of both the ASTC block
height and 4 (the BC block height). Every block sees the texels it saw before: the output is the same bytes (checked on a PC against this Eden's own
astc.cpp, bcn.cpp and stb_dxt.cpp on 430 images, every block size, BC1 and BC3, sizes that are multiples of nothing; three planted bugs were caught).

The logic is in a header of its own (video_core/textures/astc_recompress.h, master copy in patches/eden-src/ of the port) so that it can be
tested without building Eden. No change when astc_recompress is off (the decode then goes straight to the staging buffer).

